WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:04.760
VibeVideoAgent: closing the perceptual loop

2
00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:06.634
for code-driven video generation.

3
00:00:07.143 --> 00:00:10.776
Vibe Video lets coding agents write the programs behind a video.

4
00:00:10.776 --> 00:00:13.722
But exit code zero says nothing about what viewers see.

5
00:00:13.722 --> 00:00:15.809
Raw frames flood the coder's context.

6
00:00:15.809 --> 00:00:17.929
and late critique forces full rewrites.

7
00:00:18.493 --> 00:00:21.032
Our key insight: decouple seeing from coding.

8
00:00:21.032 --> 00:00:23.543
Short-lived perception workers inspect each draft

9
00:00:23.543 --> 00:00:25.999
and return compact. grounded Findings.

10
00:00:25.999 --> 00:00:29.257
so the coder can check early. without drowning in pixels.

11
00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:30.765
Here's a real run.

12
00:00:30.765 --> 00:00:32.685
The agent plans from quoted requirements

13
00:00:32.685 --> 00:00:35.464
and tests its riskiest shot on a six-second preview.

14
00:00:35.464 --> 00:00:37.305
On the rough cut. a check fails:

15
00:00:37.305 --> 00:00:39.013
the elders aren't holding hands.

16
00:00:39.013 --> 00:00:41.077
After a patch. the re-check passes.

17
00:00:41.077 --> 00:00:43.845
while compare_media flags a fix that never landed.

18
00:00:43.845 --> 00:00:46.836
Finally. a local probe confirms a seamless loop.

19
00:00:47.593 --> 00:00:50.227
On all 239 VibeVideoBench tasks.

20
00:00:50.227 --> 00:00:52.816
holistic quality improves for every coding agent.

21
00:00:52.816 --> 00:00:54.468
up to +0.40.

22
00:00:54.468 --> 00:00:56.811
with the largest gains in audio-visual sync.

23
00:00:57.510 --> 00:00:58.799
VibeVideoAgent.

24
00:00:58.799 --> 00:01:00.221
See what you render.
